From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 03:42:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: apple: ave: let the client choose the SPS
 frame_num and POC words

A VA-API client writes its own sequence parameter set into the container
header (avcC, hvcC) and expects the encoder's in-band one to match. FFmpeg
codes log2_max_frame_num_minus4 = 4 and, without B-frames,
pic_order_cnt_type = 2 (HEVC: log2_max_pic_order_cnt_lsb_minus4 = 8), while
the 9003.78.0 firmware writes the macOS constants 1, 0 and 2 (HEVC 7):
Matroska muxing then refuses the stream and players decode it with the
wrong slice header widths.

On 9003.78.0 the H.264 sequence block sits 0xdc98 bytes below the 6070
one (profile, level, max_num_ref_frames and the picture size all match),
and the stream the firmware writes carries Start +0x2d34, +0x2d38 and
+0x2d3c as log2_max_frame_num_minus4, pic_order_cnt_type and
log2_max_pic_order_cnt_lsb_minus4. The layout had none of them, so the
existing writes of these words only reached the 6070 build.

Add four driver-private integer controls, offered only on a build whose
layout knows the word. -1, the default, keeps the firmware value as macOS
sets it, so a client that does not set them gets a byte-identical Start;
otherwise the syntax element value goes into the word.

Add a test-only sps_probe parameter that sets raw Start words inside the
codec's sequence block, so the firmware's use of neighbouring words can be
swept on the hardware. It is empty by default and logs each word it sets.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
